userStories.description:
**Goal** — Persist unique PR-head claims/outcomes and add compare-and-set auto-disable with transactional audit.

**Motivation** — No component dispatches `REVIEW` jobs.

**Approach** — `FleetPrReview` columns:
- `id` (cuid);
- `repoId`, an FK to `FleetRepo` with cascade delete;
- `prNumber Int`, `headSha String`;
- `jobId String?`;
- `outcome String`;
- `createdAt DateTime @default(now())`, `updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt`.

It has a unique index on `(repoId, prNumber, headSha)`. It sits behind `FLEET_PR_REVIEW_REPOSITORY` (interface `IFleetPrReviewRepository`), implemented by `PrismaFleetPrReviewRepository extends AbstractPrismaRepository`, which adds:
- `findHead(repoId, prNumber, headSha): Promise<FleetPrReviewRecord | null>`;
- `insertPending(repoId, prNumber, headSha): Promise<FleetPrReviewRecord | null>`. It returns null on a unique violation (P2002), so a concurrent pass loses quietly;
- `markDispatched(id, jobId): Promise<void>`;
- `markFailed(id, outcome): Promise<void>`;
- `remove(id): Promise<void>`;
- `findPrincipalAccess(ref: PrincipalRef): Promise<{ exists: boolean; disabled: boolean }>`, the `PrismaScheduleRepository.findOwnerAccess` rules.

One row per PR head. `FleetPrReview` has one row per `(repoId, prNumber, headSha)`. Its `outcome` is `pending` (inserted, dispatch not finished), `dispatched` (with `jobId`) or `failed:<reason>`. A head with a `dispatched` or `failed:` row is never dispatched again.

A USER exists when its `User` row does and is disabled when `User.disabled` is true. An AGENT exists when its `Agent` row does and is never disabled. `PrismaFleetPrReviewRepository.findPrincipalAccess` reads the `User` and `Agent` tables directly, as `PrismaScheduleRepository.findOwnerAccess` does; this is the accepted precedent, not a layering breach.

Compare-and-set turn-off. `disableAutoReview(id, reason, expectedEnablerId)` changes the row only when `autoReview` is true and `autoReviewEnabledById` equals `expectedEnablerId`. It records its audit entry only when it changed the row, and returns whether it did. So a pass reading a stale repo list cannot turn off a repo someone else has just re-enabled.

`FleetReposService.disableAutoReview(id: string, reason: string, expectedEnablerId: string): Promise<boolean>` makes the call-4 compare-and-set update and records the audit entry `{ key: 'system.pr-review.dispatch', resourceType: 'fleet-repo', resourceId: id, projectId, summary: 'auto-review disabled: <reason>' }`, both inside one `txManager.run`.

`IFleetRepoRepository` adds `disableAutoReviewIf(id: string, expectedEnablerId: string): Promise<boolean>`. Disable clears autoReview and the three autoReviewEnabled* columns, preserving profiles/cost. Add migration `20261113090000_fleet_pr_review`, schema model and FleetRepo reverse relation. Put record/interface/token in reviews/domain/fleet-pr-review.domain.ts. Use transaction-scoped Prisma and generated imports; adopt existing AbstractPrismaRepository patterns, and return null only for P2002. Unit-test turn-off audit with mocks and repository behavior under the shared DB gate. The new system catalog key/writer/module are deliberately deferred together to US-009; no background writer is booted by this story.

**Scope** — In: ledger schema/migration/repository/access lookup and repo CAS/service audit methods. Out: dispatch selection/recovery and outcome policy (US-006/007), permission recheck gate (US-008), writer/key/module registration (US-009). Deleting old `FleetPrReview` rows; they are kept.

**Interface** — `IFleetPrReviewRepository` methods above; `disableAutoReviewIf(id: string, expectedEnablerId: string): Promise<boolean>`; `FleetReposService.disableAutoReview(id: string, reason: string, expectedEnablerId: string): Promise<boolean>`.
